1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,800
 In this lecture, we're going to look at the architectural decision point 9 which is observability

2
00:00:05,800 --> 00:00:07,960
 and monitoring.

3
00:00:07,960 --> 00:00:13,680
 In this lecture, we're going to look at bedrock observability and monitoring.

4
00:00:13,680 --> 00:00:20,560
 So you can monitor all aspects of your GenAI application built on Amazon bedrock using Amazon

5
00:00:20,560 --> 00:00:21,560
 CloudWatch.

6
00:00:21,560 --> 00:00:26,920
 Now, the second way to monitor your GenAI application built on Amazon bedrock is through

7
00:00:26,920 --> 00:00:27,920
 metrics.

8
00:00:27,920 --> 00:00:34,520
 So monitor the GenAI app performance using CloudWatch near real-time metrics.

9
00:00:34,520 --> 00:00:39,960
 So some of the common metrics that you may want to monitor is token count, input, and output

10
00:00:39,960 --> 00:00:48,720
 to manage cost, latency of the response, and errors that can help improve customer experience.

11
00:00:48,720 --> 00:00:53,680
 Then you can also set alarms that watch for certain thresholds and send notifications

12
00:00:53,680 --> 00:00:58,040
 or take actions when the value exceeds those thresholds.

13
00:00:58,040 --> 00:01:03,160
 So let's say you have a threshold where you want the model to generate the response within

14
00:01:03,160 --> 00:01:04,160
 two seconds.

15
00:01:04,160 --> 00:01:08,680
 So you can look at the latency of metrics and set a threshold of two seconds.

16
00:01:08,680 --> 00:01:13,480
 So if there are any responses that take more than two seconds, then it can generate

17
00:01:13,480 --> 00:01:16,240
 a alarm or take some downstream action.

18
00:01:16,240 --> 00:01:21,880
 Okay, now let's go and take a look at some of the metrics available in CloudWatch for Amazon

19
00:01:21,880 --> 00:01:22,880
 bedrock.

20
00:01:22,880 --> 00:01:24,880
 So first you have the invocation.

21
00:01:24,880 --> 00:01:26,800
 So the unit will be sample count.

22
00:01:26,800 --> 00:01:32,040
 And here you can take a look at model invocation logging where you can collect invocation logs,

23
00:01:32,040 --> 00:01:38,360
 model input, data basically prompt, output data for all the invocation.

24
00:01:38,360 --> 00:01:42,240
 Then second we have the invocation latency, which is measured in milliseconds.

25
00:01:42,240 --> 00:01:44,720
 And here you can track the latency of the invocation.

26
00:01:44,720 --> 00:01:49,160
 So this is important as we discussed for customer experience, you can track the latency of

27
00:01:49,160 --> 00:01:50,160
 the invocation.

28
00:01:50,160 --> 00:01:54,240
 Then you can track the invocation client errors, invocation server errors, and you can

29
00:01:54,240 --> 00:01:56,280
 get the sample count.

30
00:01:56,280 --> 00:02:02,320
 Then you can look at invocation throttling again, number invocation that the system throttled.

31
00:02:02,320 --> 00:02:07,040
 So if there is a lot of throttling, then you can look at cross region inferencing.

32
00:02:07,040 --> 00:02:08,800
 Then you have the input token count.

33
00:02:08,800 --> 00:02:13,200
 Again, this is important metrics if you want to track the cost.

34
00:02:13,200 --> 00:02:17,360
 And for any production grid application, this is a really important metrics.

35
00:02:17,360 --> 00:02:22,660
 Then your legacy model invocation, if you're using a legacy model, let's say you are using

36
00:02:22,660 --> 00:02:27,560
 a legacy model from entropic or any other model product, then you can track that.

37
00:02:27,560 --> 00:02:32,480
 Then you have the output token count and output image count.

38
00:02:32,480 --> 00:02:36,520
 Again this can be used for cost tracking.

39
00:02:36,520 --> 00:02:39,400
 Then you can finally also track the output image count.

40
00:02:39,400 --> 00:02:45,600
 So if your model is generating images, then you can track this metric as well.

41
00:02:45,600 --> 00:02:49,800
 And now you can also monitor Amazon bedrock guardrails using cloud watch metrics.

42
00:02:49,800 --> 00:02:55,880
 So you can see your invocation number request to apply guardrails API operation.

43
00:02:55,880 --> 00:02:58,400
 Invocation latency, so latency of the invocation.

44
00:02:58,400 --> 00:03:04,920
 So if you're using guardrails, you can check if it is increasing the latency for your

45
00:03:04,920 --> 00:03:05,920
 invocation.

46
00:03:05,920 --> 00:03:11,840
 So there will be some latency from the model, but guardrails can also add on to that latency.

47
00:03:11,840 --> 00:03:16,200
 So you can see here, there are different metrics that you can track using cloud watch metrics

48
00:03:16,200 --> 00:03:18,040
 for guardrails.

49
00:03:18,040 --> 00:03:22,400
 Now just to summarize, there are three different ways in which you can monitor your journey

50
00:03:22,400 --> 00:03:24,480
 applications using cloud watch.

51
00:03:24,480 --> 00:03:25,640
 So first is the metrics.

52
00:03:25,640 --> 00:03:28,320
 So we've already looked at different cloud watch metrics.

53
00:03:28,320 --> 00:03:33,640
 Then we have the logs, cloud watch logs, and we saw that there is full invocation logging,

54
00:03:33,640 --> 00:03:36,840
 input prompt, output, and a lot of metadata.

55
00:03:36,840 --> 00:03:42,560
 And then finally, you can also use log queries for creating a dashboard or getting custom

56
00:03:42,560 --> 00:03:43,560
 insights.

57
00:03:43,560 --> 00:03:50,360
 And this can be done using cloud watch log insights, so query and analyze logs, not metrics.

58
00:03:50,360 --> 00:03:52,520
 So here's an example that I was trying out.

59
